####### Git-Ops + Declarative Streaming (metadata/ + medallion_pipelines.yml)

enterprise-data-platform/
├── databricks.yml                  # Root DAB configuration (targets: dev, staging, prod)
├── pyproject.toml                  # Python dependencies & wheel build config
├── metadata/                       # Metadata configs (synced to control_catalog or Volumes)
│   ├── control_tables/             # DDL for control_catalog (pipeline_config, dq_rules)
│   └── pipelines/
│       ├── sales_domain.yaml       # Source-to-target mappings, watermarks, DQ expectations
│       └── customer_domain.yaml
├── resources/                      # DAB resource definitions (Jobs, Pipelines, Schemas)
│   ├── catalogs_and_schemas.yml    # Unity Catalog definitions (bronze, silver, gold, control)
│   ├── ingestion_workflows.yml     # Databricks Workflows calling the ingestion engine
│   └── medallion_pipelines.yml     # Lakeflow Declarative Pipelines (DLT) definitions
├── src/
│   └── platform_core/              # Reusable Python wheel package
│       ├── __init__.py
│       ├── config_loader.py        # Reads metadata from control_catalog or YAML
│       ├── dq_engine.py            # Applies dynamic Data Quality rules
│       ├── bronze_ingestor.py      # Metadata-driven Auto Loader engine
│       └── silver_transformer.py   # Dynamic deduplication, SCD Type 1/2, & casting
└── tests/                          # Pytest & Chispa unit tests
    ├── test_bronze_ingestor.py
    └── test_silver_transformer.py

• When it is useful:

    • Real-time or continuous streaming: You need low-latency ingestion using Auto Loader and Lakeflow Declarative Pipelines (dlt) rather than scheduled batch scripts.
    • Built-in DLT Data Quality & Lineage: You want Databricks to natively manage checkpointing, automatic retries, SCD Type 1/2 (APPLY CHANGES), and visual DLT expectation metrics without writing custom Python merge code.
    • Strict Git-Ops governance: Every schema mapping or data quality rule change must go through a Git Pull Request, peer review, and automated CI/CD checks before reaching production.

• Trade-off: Does not fit pure batch API polling well, and DLT compute carries higher licensing costs than standard Jobs compute.


## Git-Ops + Batch Workflows (metadata/ + batch_workflows.yml)

enterprise-data-platform/
├── databricks.yml                  # Root DAB config (targets: dev, staging, prod)
├── pyproject.toml                  # Python wheel build & dependencies (requests, tenacity, pyyaml)
├── metadata/
│   ├── control_ddl/
│   │   └── setup_control_tables.sql # DDLs for pipeline_config, watermarks, & audit_logs
│   └── pipelines/
│       ├── erp_adls_sales.yaml     # ADLS batch file ingestion & Silver/Gold rules
│       └── crm_api_customers.yaml  # REST API batch ingestion & Silver/Gold rules
├── resources/
│   ├── catalogs.yml                # Unity Catalogs: bronze_catalog, silver_catalog, gold_catalog, control_catalog
│   └── batch_workflows.yml         # Scheduled Databricks Jobs (Bronze -> Silver -> Gold tasks)
├── src/
│   └── platform_core/
│       ├── __init__.py
│       ├── watermark_manager.py    # Reads/updates incremental watermarks in control_catalog
│       ├── adls_batch_ingestor.py  # Batch file reader (Parquet/CSV/JSON) via UC External Locations
│       ├── api_batch_ingestor.py   # Resilient REST API client (retries, pagination, Secrets)
│       ├── silver_batch_engine.py  # Batch deduplication, DQ checks, & Delta MERGE (SCD1/SCD2)
│       └── gold_batch_engine.py    # SQL/PySpark dimensional & fact table aggregations
└── tests/
    ├── test_api_ingestor.py
    └── test_silver_merge.py


• When it is useful:
	• Small-to-medium batch platforms (under ~150 tables): You run scheduled batch jobs for ADLS files and REST APIs, and your team prefers storing configurations in Git rather than managing a SQL metadata table.
	• Auditability via Pull Requests: Financial or regulated teams that require a Git commit hash and peer approval every time an API endpoint, primary key, or DQ rule changes.
	• Easy environment promotion: Promoting a new pipeline from dev to staging to prod happens automatically when merging a Git branch, without needing SQL migration scripts to sync rows across environments.
• Trade-off: Once you exceed 150–200 tables, maintaining dozens of domain YAML files in Git becomes cumbersome compared to Structure 1.


#### Pure Database-Driven (bootstrap/ + metadata_batch_job.yml) True metadata driven 

enterprise-data-platform/
├── databricks.yml                      # Root DAB configuration (dev, staging, prod)
├── pyproject.toml                      # Python wheel build config
├── bootstrap/                          # Run once during environment setup
│   └── 01_create_control_tables.sql    # DDLs for pipeline_metadata, watermarks, & audit_logs
├── resources/
│   ├── catalogs.yml                    # bronze_catalog, silver_catalog, gold_catalog, control_catalog
│   └── metadata_batch_job.yml          # Single generic Databricks Job orchestrating all pipelines
├── src/
│   └── platform_core/
│       ├── __init__.py
│       ├── main.py                     # CLI entry points (run_bronze, run_silver, run_gold)
│       ├── metadata_repository.py      # Queries control_catalog.governance.pipeline_metadata
│       ├── adls_ingestor.py            # Generic ADLS batch reader
│       ├── api_ingestor.py             # Generic REST API batch reader
│       ├── silver_engine.py            # Generic DQ, deduplication, & Delta MERGE engine
│       └── gold_engine.py              # Executes dynamic Gold SQL/transformations from metadata
└── tests/
    └── test_metadata_engine.py

• When it is useful:
	• High-volume table onboarding (100+ tables): You ingest hundreds of ERP tables, ADLS folders, or APIs and do not want to manage hundreds of YAML files in Git.
	• Self-service or UI-driven onboarding: Non-engineers, data stewards, or an internal web portal onboard new data sources by inserting rows into control_catalog.governance.pipeline_metadata.
	• Dynamic scheduling: You want to pause, resume, or change data quality rules for a single table immediately via SQL without waiting for a CI/CD code deployment.
• Trade-off: Because metadata lives in a database rather than Git, you must rely on Delta table history (DESCRIBE HISTORY) and strict Unity Catalog RBAC permissions to track who changed a rule.




+---------------------------+-------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------+
| Architecture Style        | Structure 1: Pure Database-Driven         | Structure 2: GitOps + Declarative         | Structure 3: GitOps + Batch Workflows     |
|                           | (bootstrap/ + metadata_batch_job.yml)     | Streaming (metadata/ +                    | (metadata/ + batch_workflows.yml)         |
|                           |                                           | medallion_pipelines.yml)                  |                                           |
+---------------------------+-------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------+
| Where Metadata Lives      | Inside Delta Tables                       | Inside Git YAML files                    | Inside Git YAML files                     |
|                           | (control_catalog)                         |                                           |                                           |
+---------------------------+-------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------+
| Execution Engine          | Standard Batch PySpark Jobs               | Lakeflow Declarative Pipelines (DLT)     | Standard Batch PySpark Jobs               |
+---------------------------+-------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------+
| How New Tables Are Added  | Run INSERT / MERGE SQL query              | Create a PR with a new/updated YAML file | Create a PR with a new/updated YAML file |
|                           | (or use a UI/Admin app)                   |                                           |                                           |
+---------------------------+-------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------+
| Best Scale                | 100 to 5,000+ tables                      | 10 to 200 tables                         | 10 to 200 tables                          |
+---------------------------+-------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------+


