Databricks recommends yaml configs , so final one generated with best of all is 
Databricks officially recommends Approach 1 (Git-Ops with Databricks Asset Bundles) as the modern standard for most data engineering teams, 
though Approach 2 (Database-Driven) remains the standard for massive, multi-hundred-table enterprise migrations.


COMPARISON: EASY vs. ROBUST vs. SCALABLE

Factor                     | Approach 1: Git-Ops (YAML + DAB)                                      | Approach 2: Database-Driven (pipeline_metadata)
---------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------
Easiest to Build &         | WINNER. Zero custom orchestration code needed.                         | Harder. Requires building and maintaining a metadata
Maintain                   | Databricks natively handles scheduling, retries, and                  | repository, audit logging, concurrency handling,
                           | task dependencies.                                                    | and SQL migration scripts.

Most Robust                | WINNER. Each pipeline runs as an isolated Databricks                  | More fragile. If 1 of 50 tables fails inside a Python
(Failure Handling)         | Job/Task. Failures are clearly visible in the UI, and                 | loop, custom error handling is required to prevent
                           | "Repair Run" can retry only the failed table.                         | the entire batch from failing or masking errors.

UI Observability           | WINNER. Databricks Workflows UI provides run duration,                | Limited. Databricks shows only a generic task
                           | Spark UI links, status, and row-level visibility for                 | (execute_all_bronze). Detailed troubleshooting
                           | individual pipelines/tables.                                          | requires querying audit_logs tables.

Adding 500+ Tables         | Becomes cluttered. Managing hundreds of YAML files                    | WINNER. Scaling is simple. Adding 500 tables
                           | and Databricks Jobs can create deployment and UI                      | only requires inserting 500 new metadata records
                           | management challenges.                                                | into a Delta table with no code deployment.

Scalability                | Good for small-to-medium implementations where                        | Excellent for large-scale implementations with
                           | pipeline definitions are managed as code.                             | hundreds or thousands of tables.

Operational Overhead       | Low. Databricks platform handles orchestration and                    | High. Requires ongoing maintenance of metadata,
                           | monitoring capabilities out of the box.                               | framework logic, audits, and orchestration rules.

Flexibility                | Changes typically require updating YAML files and                     | Highly flexible. New pipelines can be added or
                           | redeploying bundles.                                                  | modified through metadata changes only.

Recommended Usage          | Best for organizations prioritizing simplicity,                       | Best for enterprise-scale data platforms requiring
                           | governance, CI/CD, and operational supportability.                    | dynamic configuration and massive scalability.

Overall Verdict            | BEST FOR EASE OF IMPLEMENTATION AND OPERATIONS.                       | BEST FOR LARGE-SCALE, METADATA-DRIVEN PLATFORMS.


